            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.732,
            0.064,
            0.834,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Prime Time"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.12,
            0.756,
            0.14
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9. Identify whether each statement is true or false. Explain."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.143,
            0.714,
            0.162
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "a. There is no prime number whose units digit is 4."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.166,
            0.622,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "b. A product of primes can also be prime."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.189,
            0.634,
            0.208
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "c. Prime numbers do not have any factors."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.211,
            0.656,
            0.23
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "d. All even numbers are composite numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.234,
            0.833,
            0.276
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "e. 2 is a prime and so is the next number, 3. For every other prime, the next number is composite."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.279,
            0.833,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "10. Which of the following numbers is the product of exactly three distinct prime numbers: 45, 60, 91, 105, 330?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.325,
            0.834,
            0.367
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11. How many three-digit prime numbers can you make using each of 2, 4 and 5 once?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.37,
            0.833,
            0.437
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "12. Observe that 3 is a prime number, and 2 × 3 + 1 = 7 is also a prime. Are there other primes for which doubling and adding 1 gives another prime? Find at least five such examples."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.462,
            0.825,
            0.488
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5.3 Co-prime numbers for safekeeping treasures"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.504,
            0.365,
            0.526
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Which pairs are safe?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.534,
            0.834,
            0.629
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us go back to the treasure finding game. This time, treasures are kept on two numbers. Jumpy gets the treasures only if he is able to reach both the numbers with the same jump size. There is also a new rule — a jump size of 1 is not allowed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.636,
            0.833,
            0.68
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Where should Grumpy place the treasures so that Jumpy cannot reach both the treasures?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.69,
            0.833,
            0.736
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Will placing the treasure on 12 and 26 work? No! If the jump size is chosen to be 2, then Jumpy will reach both 12 and 26."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.739,
            0.833,
            0.784
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What about 4 and 9? Jumpy cannot reach both using any jump size other than 1. So, Grumpy knows that the pair 4 and 9 is safe."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.786,
            0.454,
            0.806
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Check if these pairs are safe:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.808,
            0.476,
            0.828
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "a. 15 and 39 b. 4 and 15"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.831,
            0.487,
            0.852
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "c. 18 and 29 d. 20 and 55"
            }
          ]
        },
